  "body": "### Summary\n\nAs a user tapping a formatting button with something in the clipboard, I want the question to offer named choices and a real « Annuler », so that I can back out of the tap and Esc on a keyboard inserts nothing.\n\n### Rule\n\n- \"Use an action sheet — not an alert — to offer choices related to an intentional action.\" and \"In informational alerts only, you can use \"OK\" for acceptance, avoiding \"Yes\" and \"No.\" Always use \"Cancel\" to title a button that cancels the alert's action.\" — [HIG › Alerts](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/alerts) (page updated 2024-02-02).\n- \"Provide alternative ways to cancel an alert when it makes sense.\" — same page.\n\n### What the app does (dev-swift @ `7a61018f`)\n\n- `App/SwiftHFR/Composer/ReplyComposerView.swift:105-109`: `.alert(\"Utiliser le presse-papiers ?\" …)`.\n- `:348-361`: `.link` → « Non » (`role: .cancel`) / « Oui »; `.linkChoice` → « Sans le presse-papiers » (`role: .cancel`) / « Oui [url=XXX] » / « Oui [url]XXX[/url] »; `.formatting` → « Sans le presse-papiers » (`role: .cancel`) / « Insérer ».\n- The cancel-role button is not a cancel: it calls `formatting.declinePending(pending)` (`Composer/ComposerFormattingModel.swift:114-121`), which applies the tag without the clipboard. No button backs out of the tap, and the system's cancel gestures (Esc, ⌘.) trigger the cancel-role button, so they insert the tag.\n\n### Legacy at `87fe0d1c`\n\nSame alert, titles and roles: `HFRswift/Swift/AnswerView.swift:530-557`.\n\n### Class\n\n**(b) evolution, M5** (same as legacy).\n\n### Proposed fix and check\n\n- A `confirmationDialog` with verb titles (« Coller le lien », « Insérer sans le presse-papiers », …) and a real « Annuler » (`role: .cancel`) that only dismisses the pending question.\n- Check: a model test that the cancel path leaves the text unchanged. Mac check: Esc on a hardware keyboard inserts nothing.\n\nRelated: #1151 (the other « Oui » / « Non » alerts of this review).\n\nFound by #866 (cloud/r866-fable, Fable 5.2)",
